From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0344-driver-04-vma-block-size.patch

Source: 0344-driver-04-vma-block-size.patch
Core patch: for tools/eglantine-patches, any number after 0181 (the mesh-compute patches also use
0180 and 0181). Paths are the submodule's. Applies on core16 (f9ab11e54f).

Holes inside VMA's blocks came to 172-259 MiB across the refusals of runs 57 and 61 (247 of 3315 in
606 holes, the largest 7 MiB, at run 61's first). The comment on this setting records holes shrinking
with block size down to 16 MiB. Anything over half a block gets memory of its own, in 16 KiB pages.

Switch: EDEN_VMA_BLOCK_MIB=<n>, 2 to 64; unset keeps 16. Try 8. Only the default blocks, which hold the
images; the separate pools stay at 16 MiB, and the logs do not say how many of the holes are theirs.
Cost: more blocks and more dedicated allocations, each two system calls, in a game that makes and
frees images by the thousand. A hole may only move: a dedicated image of 2 MiB or more starts on a
2 MiB boundary (radv_ps5_memory_alloc), so one of 5 MiB can leave up to 1 MiB of the pool that a
2 MiB block cannot use. Watch EDEN_REFUSED_POOL's free blocks beside EDEN_VMA_USAGE.

Guess: some of the holes come back, how many is not known. The added lines compile with VMA 3.3.0;
vulkan_device.cpp as a whole not compiled (no Boost.Unordered or fmt here), not run.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/src/video_core/vulkan_common/vulkan_device.cpp b/src/video_core/vulkan_common/vulkan_device.cpp
index 89a939bf2b..d9d0117f05 100644
--- a/src/video_core/vulkan_common/vulkan_device.cpp
+++ b/src/video_core/vulkan_common/vulkan_device.cpp
@@ -795,6 +795,17 @@ Device::Device(VkInstance instance_, vk::PhysicalDevice physical_, VkSurfaceKHR
             }
         }
     }
+    // EDEN_VMA_BLOCK_MIB=<n>, 2 to 64: blocks of that size. Smaller blocks drain sooner and leave
+    // fewer holes, as the note below found going from 256 to 16 MiB, and everything over half a
+    // block gets memory of its own. On the console the holes are the pool's: 172 to 259 MiB of it in
+    // about 600 pieces when Zelda's allocations were refused (runs 57, 61).
+    VkDeviceSize vma_block_bytes = 16u * 1024u * 1024u;
+    if (const char* value = std::getenv("EDEN_VMA_BLOCK_MIB")) {
+        const unsigned long mib = std::strtoul(value, nullptr, 10);
+        if (mib >= 2 && mib <= 64) {
+            vma_block_bytes = VkDeviceSize{mib} << 20;
+        }
+    }
     const VmaAllocatorCreateInfo allocator_info{
             .flags = flags,
             .physicalDevice = physical,
@@ -806,7 +817,7 @@ Device::Device(VkInstance instance_, vk::PhysicalDevice physical_, VkSurfaceKHR
             // after ten minutes, with 32 MiB ones ~0.5 GB: a block drains only once all of its
             // images are gone, and fewer per block drain sooner. VMA gives allocations over half a
             // block (8 MiB) their own memory, which goes back to the driver when they are freed.
-            .preferredLargeHeapBlockSize = 16u * 1024u * 1024u,
+            .preferredLargeHeapBlockSize = vma_block_bytes,
             .pAllocationCallbacks = nullptr,
             .pDeviceMemoryCallbacks = nullptr,
             .pHeapSizeLimit = heap_size_limit != 0 ? heap_size_limits.data() : nullptr,
